    <!-- Add Activity -->
     <axiom component="lesson-plan">
      <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
      <dl>
        <li>
          <title>Suggested Time</title>
          <p>15 minutes</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Objective</title>
          <p>To realize that fractions are easier to compare and compute when the denominator is a rational number.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <title>Teacher's Role</title>
          <p>Write <m>\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}</m> on the board. Ask learners to estimate its value using a calculator. Then ask them to multiply both the numerator and denominator by <m>\sqrt{2}</m> and estimate again. Lead a discussion on why <m>\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}</m> might be more <q>useful</q> than <m>\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}</m>.</p>
        </li>
      </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity>
      <li>
                <title>Group Structure</title>
                <p>Learners work in collaborative groups of 3 to 4.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Materials Needed</title>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>Standard scientific calculators.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Flipcharts, manila papers, or small portable whiteboards.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Markers of different colors.</p></li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </li>
     <statement>
            <p>
                 In groups of 3 to 4, investigate how multiplying by specialized forms of 1 changes the presentation of a fraction without altering its value.
            </p>
            
            <p><alert>Group Investigation</alert></p>
            <ol>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Consider the fraction <m>\frac{3}{\sqrt{5}}</m>
                        If you multiply it by <m>\frac{\sqrt{5}}{\sqrt{5}}</m> what happens to the radical in the denominator? Show your step-by-step calculations.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Use a scientific calculator to evaluate the decimal value of the original fraction and your new fraction. Why does multiplying by <m>\frac{\sqrt{n}}{\sqrt{n}}</m> not change the value of the fraction?Explain the mathematical reasoning behind your observation.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Now, look at a more complex expression: <me>\frac{1}{2+\sqrt{3}}</me>
                        Try to find a strategic way to completely remove the radical from this denominator. 
                        <hint><p>Remember the difference of two squares identity from your previous algebra lessons: <m>(a-b)(a+b) = a^2 - b^2</m>.</p></hint>
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ol>
            <p>
                Once your group finishes tasks 1 through 3, write out your final solutions and explanations clearly on your flipchart or manila paper. Participate in the following sharing session:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        One member of your group will "stay" at your station as the Ambassador to explain your group's strategy and how you handled the hint in Task 3.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The remaining group members will "stray" to visit at least two neighboring groups to check their work. 
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Compare your methods for Task 3. Did other groups choose the same terms to multiply? Critically evaluate their work, ask questions, and give feedback before returning to your home group to consolidate your insights.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </statement>
    </activity>

