<axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>

        <table>
            <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
            <tabular halign="left">
                <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Learner Group</cell>
                    <cell>Strategy &amp; Learning Experience</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        Provide numerical benchmarks using simple values like <m>\frac{1}{\sqrt{4}}</m> versus <m>\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}</m> to ground the concept. Guide them to focus exclusively on monomial denominators first, using structured worksheets where the "special form of 1" (such as <m>\frac{\sqrt{3}}{\sqrt{3}}</m>) is explicitly highlighted as a starting step.
                    </cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        The core lesson activities as described above, including the group discovery task, investigating conjugates for binomial expressions, and practicing geometric or real-world application problems.
                    </cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        Challenge learners to discover how to rationalise trinomial denominators, such as <m>\frac{1}{1 + \sqrt{2} + \sqrt{3}}</m>, by applying the grouping strategy and multiplying by conjugates twice. Alternatively, have them explore the algebraic generalization of rationalising cubic roots using the identity for the sum or difference of two cubes.
                    </cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>
